# .audit-patterns.example
#
# Format: one extended-regex per line. Lines starting with # are comments.
# The CI workflow greps the whole tree (except node_modules / .git /
# test/fixtures) with these patterns. Any match fails the build.
#
# Two ways to use this:
#
# 1. LOCAL audit — copy this file to .audit-patterns (gitignored),
#    fill in your patterns, and run:
#
#        grep -rEf .audit-patterns . \
#          --exclude-dir=node_modules \
#          --exclude-dir=.git \
#          --exclude-dir=test/fixtures
#
#    The CI workflow does the same thing.
#
# 2. CI audit — configure the same patterns as a GitHub repository
#    secret named AUDIT_PATTERNS (Settings → Secrets and variables →
#    Actions → New repository secret). One extended-regex per line.
#    Secret is exposed to main-branch and internal-PR runs; for
#    security, GitHub does not expose secrets to fork PRs.
#
# Suggested categories — replace placeholders with your own:

PLACEHOLDER_USERNAME_1
PLACEHOLDER_USERNAME_2
PLACEHOLDER_OLD_EMAIL@example\.com
PLACEHOLDER_UUID_PREFIX-[0-9a-f-]+
